{
  "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
  "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: I could understand that if the plan is being set up or if there's going to be a change to the multiemployer plan while the business is ongoing. But in this situation, you, you say if the employer elects to have an annuity, then choosing which insurance company is going to supply the annuity, that would be a fiduciary function."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well if you're correct and this was a sponsor decision, not a fiduciary decision, let me ask you when you're wearing, when the company is wearing its sponsor hat and says we're going to terminate this plan, does it have a duty to consider the best interests and the security of the employees, number one, when it picks an insurance company?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Could an administrator make that decision in its fiduciary capacity?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Was that position presented, I should have asked you --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court of Appeals",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0001-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court of Appeals held that Petitioner had waived the argument, based on the terms of the plan, because Petitioner hadn't made that argument in the bankruptcy court, even though the district court had actually addressed the terms of the plan, but mistakenly construed the plan to permit merger, Your Honor."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Of course this argument would not have any force whatever if indeed, transferring the assets to another plan does not constitute a termination of the plan."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondents",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1448",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "def1d97cb63ccc9cd13d7b34d8ed118c73f20ff4f8255275074a1b080675567e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-def1d97cb63c-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. CLARK: The Government's regulations on single employer plan mergers take the very clear position, and we cited them in our brief, it's the regulations under Section 414(1), the clear position that any time there is a transfer of assets and liabilities from one plan to another, whether a complete transfer or not, that is treated as a spinoff of a plan from the original plan and a merger of the spun off assets and liabilities into the other plan."
    },
    {
